---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { factorBlurbs, factorLabels, meta, methodology, momentumLabels, sources } from "../lib/data";
import { label, describe } from "../lib/format";
const site = meta.site;
const bands = Object.entries(methodology.grading.bands).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const mo = methodology.momentum;
const moBands = Object.entries(mo.grading.bands).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const risingAt = mo.grading.bands.rising;
const steadyAt = mo.grading.bands.steady;
const scoredInputs = mo.factors.flatMap((f) => f.inputs.filter((i) => !i.context_only)).length;
const neededInputs = Math.ceil(mo.min_coverage * scoredInputs);
const jsonLd = {
  "@type": "Dataset",
  "@id": new URL("/methodology#dataset", Astro.site).toString(),
  name: `${site.SITE_NAME} readiness and momentum dataset: ${meta.town_count} New York towns`,
  alternateName: `${site.SITE_NAME} dataset`,
  keywords: ["New York towns", "Hudson Valley", "Capital Region", "Mohawk Valley", "readiness score", "momentum", "revitalization", "home values", "building permits", "population change", "county migration", "Downtown Revitalization Initiative"],
  version: `readiness ${methodology.version}, momentum ${mo.version}`,
  temporalCoverage: `2019/${meta.generated_at.slice(0, 4)}`,
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  measurementTechnique: "Each metric is taken from a named public file, kept with its period and margin of error; ACS ratios use the Census proportion formula and are suppressed when the margin of error exceeds 40% of the estimate. Readiness is a weighted sum of six factor means; momentum is each change judged against a New York benchmark.",
  variableMeasured: Object.entries(methodology.metrics).map(([name, m]) => ({ "@type": "PropertyValue", name: m.label, alternateName: name, description: m.description })),
  description: `Readiness scores, grades, momentum scores and labels, and every input metric with source and as-of date for ${meta.town_count} New York cities and villages.`,
  url: new URL("/methodology", Astro.site).toString(),
  license: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
  creator: { "@type": "Organization", name: site.SITE_NAME, url: `https://${site.SITE_DOMAIN}` },
  dateModified: meta.generated_at.slice(0, 10),
  spatialCoverage: "Hudson Valley, Capital Region and Mohawk Valley, New York, USA",
  distribution: [{ "@type": "DataDownload", encodingFormat: "text/csv", contentUrl: new URL("/downloads/comebacktowns-dataset.csv", Astro.site).toString() }],
  isBasedOn: sources.map((s) => s.url).filter(Boolean),
};
---
<Base title="Methodology" description={`How the readiness and momentum scores work for ${meta.town_count} New York towns: six weighted factors, every curve, rising and fading labels, the 40% suppression rule, and the CC BY 4.0 dataset download.`} jsonLd={jsonLd} crumbs={[{ name: "Methodology", path: "/methodology" }]}>
  <div class="wrap">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">Readiness asks one question: are the ingredients for a revival present? It is slow-moving and graded A to F. Momentum asks a second: is anything actually happening? It is a score with three labels. This page is written so a town official can argue with either, line by line.</p>

  <h2 id="download">The dataset</h2>
  <p><a href="/downloads/comebacktowns-dataset.csv">Download the full dataset (CSV)</a>: one row per town with every metric, its period and suppression flag, the readiness score and grade, and the momentum score and label. Licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>; the underlying sources keep their own terms, listed on <a href="/sources">Sources</a>. Generated {meta.generated_at.slice(0, 10)} with methodology version {methodology.version}.</p>

  <h2 id="factors">Six factors and their weights</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th class="num">Weight</th><th>What it asks</th><th>Inputs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {methodology.factors.map((f) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{factorLabels[f.name]}</strong></td>
            <td class="num">{Math.round(f.weight * 100)}%</td>
            <td>{factorBlurbs[f.name]}</td>
            <td class="small">{f.inputs.map((i) => label(i.metric)).join(", ")}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>A factor's score is the mean of its usable inputs, each normalised to 0–1 by the curve below. Readiness is the weighted sum of factor scores, on a 0–100 scale, renormalised over the factors that have at least one usable input.</p>

  <h2 id="curves">Every curve</h2>
  <p>Each input passes through one named curve. If a curve cannot be explained in a sentence it is wrong, so here is the sentence.</p>
  {methodology.factors.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{factorLabels[f.name]}</h3>
      <ul>
        {f.inputs.map((i) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{label(i.metric)}</strong> <span class="muted small">({describe(i.metric)})</span><br />
            {i.context_only ? <span class="muted">Used as the denominator for price headroom; it has no curve of its own.</span> : i.curve}
            {i.conditional && <span class="muted"> Only applies to towns that have won an award; its absence does not reduce coverage.</span>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2 id="grades">Grades</h2>
  <p>Grades are curved within population bands, because Census sample quality differs sharply between small villages and cities. Towns under {methodology.grading.population_band_split.toLocaleString("en-US")} residents are ranked against each other; towns at or above it likewise. Within a band the percentile cut-offs are:</p>
  <ul>{bands.map(([g, p]) => <li><strong>{g}</strong>: {p === 0 ? "everything below the D cut-off" : `${p}th percentile and above`}</li>)}</ul>

  <h2 id="momentum">Momentum</h2>
  <p>Momentum is a separate score, version {mo.version}, built by the same engine from changes over time. It is not curved against other towns: every input is judged against a benchmark, so a town keeping pace scores 0.5 on each curve and 50 overall. Home values and population are compared with the median New York place in the same source file, and tax-filer migration with the median New York county, so a statewide boom or drain does not read as momentum everywhere; permits are compared with the town's own rate two years earlier. Migration is a county figure, because the IRS publishes nothing finer: every town in a county carries the same one, and the page says so. Rent is shown on town pages but not scored, because Zillow publishes a rent index for few villages.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th class="num">Weight</th><th>What it asks</th><th>Inputs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {mo.factors.map((f) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{factorLabels[f.name]}</strong></td>
            <td class="num">{Math.round(f.weight * 100)}%</td>
            <td>{factorBlurbs[f.name]}</td>
            <td class="small">{f.inputs.map((i) => label(i.metric)).join(", ")}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  {mo.factors.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{factorLabels[f.name]}</h3>
      <ul>
        {f.inputs.map((i) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{label(i.metric)}</strong> <span class="muted small">({describe(i.metric)})</span><br />
            {i.context_only ? <span class="muted">The benchmark another input is measured against; it has no curve of its own.</span> : i.curve}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  ))}
  <h3 id="momentum-labels">Labels</h3>
  <p>The labels are absolute cut-offs on the 0–100 momentum score, not percentiles, because momentum measures direction rather than rank:</p>
  <ul>{moBands.map(([name, cutoff]) => <li><strong>{momentumLabels[name] ?? name}</strong>: {cutoff === 0 ? `below ${steadyAt}` : name === "rising" ? `${risingAt} and above` : `${cutoff} up to ${risingAt}`}</li>)}</ul>
  <p id="momentum-coverage">Below {Math.round(mo.min_coverage * 100)}% coverage, which means fewer than {neededInputs} of the {scoredInputs} scored inputs, the town's page shows its data and <strong>no label</strong>. Permit changes are suppressed when any of the five years involved was reported for fewer than twelve months, and Zillow does not cover every village; both count as missing, never as zero.</p>

  <h2 id="coverage">Coverage and missing inputs</h2>
  <p id="missing">A missing input never scores zero. It is left out of its factor, and the town's <em>coverage</em> (usable inputs over applicable inputs) drops. Below {Math.round(methodology.min_coverage * 100)}% coverage the page is published with its data and <strong>no grade</strong>, because a grade on that little evidence would be a guess.</p>

  <h2 id="suppression">Suppression</h2>
  <p>{methodology.suppression.text}</p>
  <p>A suppressed value is not the same as a missing one: the estimate exists but its margin of error is too wide to stand on. The site says "not available at this sample size" for the first and "not available" for the second, and never shows a zero for either.</p>

  <h2 id="bounds">Sanity bounds</h2>
  <p>Before anything is published, every metric is checked against a wide sanity range; a value outside it stops the run rather than publishing a unit or parsing mistake.</p>
  <details><summary class="muted">Show the ranges</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Low</th><th class="num">High</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      {Object.entries(methodology.bounds).map(([k, [lo, hi]]) => <tr><td>{label(k)}</td><td class="num">{lo}</td><td class="num">{hi}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody></table></div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="known-limits">Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Broadband is a subscription share from the Census, not a speed tier; open speed data sits behind a login we do not use.</li>
    <li>Drive times are off-peak routed estimates over OpenStreetMap; straight-line distances are kept as separate figures and never substituted.</li>
    <li>State downtown awards are read from ny.gov program pages; an amount is taken from the page only when it matches a size the programs actually pay, otherwise the program's standard award is recorded.</li>
    <li>Zillow does not cover every village. Where it does not, the figure is missing, not estimated.</li>
    <li>OpenStreetMap completeness varies by town, and the main-street factor inherits it: a town whose shops are not yet mapped scores low there until they are. Corrections belong in OpenStreetMap itself, and the next monthly refresh picks them up.</li>
    <li>Momentum's migration input is a county figure, the finest the IRS publishes, so it cannot tell one town in a county from another. Address-vacancy data from the USPS is released by HUD to governmental and non-profit users only, so it is not used; any future addition is a new momentum version, not a silent change to this one.</li>
  </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
